---
title: Tightening The Loop
publishedDate: 2026-11-03
excerpt: >-
  Two weeks back on ChromaClack with the same tools I used for TapRep, and what
  changed when I tightened the loop between planning, commits and code review.
---
> *If you want to find the truth, you have to be willing to dig for it. Usually, it's buried under layers of deception and misunderstanding. — Peter F. Hamilton, Judas Unchained*

I left my last sprint on [TapRap](https://github.com/barryodev/taprep) with a lot of optimism about using Claude CLI, Opus 5.5 and Linear. But I felt I had changed too much at once to be sure it was a rock solid improvement in my process. So I decided to keep everything in the process the same, but go back to [ChromaClacks](https://github.com/barryodev/chromaclack) and attempt what I'd failed to implement before. I spent about 8 days working on this, with a lot of time also dedicated to CV and interview prep, which is about the same time frame I spent on it a month ago. This would be a true test of whether the lessons learned and the process changes made were truly an improvement, and not just the working on a simpler problem.

Based on the lessons learned from the previous sprints, these were the principals I applied.

- **Simplify the platform**. write the prototype in a pure JS/CSS/HTML implementation, only focused on getting the math of the physics simulation correct and animation behaviour correct.
- **Keep the context small.** Use /clear in Claude CLI at the start of each issue. Spend time planning the implementation, save that plan to Linear as a checklist of commits. Before closing each session check if anything needs to be saved for the future.
- **Don't trust generated test.** The previous failed branches had hundreds of unit tests that were passing with full coverage, without providing any real value. Human checks of behaviour before every commit.
- **Establish shared nomenclature.** In the previous iteration, the retrospective identified drift of the meaning of terms in the code and design docs. This impacted the accuracy of prompt writing and generated results. Created a [GLOSSARY.md](https://github.com/barryodev/chromaclack/blob/main/GLOSSARY.md) with all concepts and terms defined. Select terms that are easy to understand and don't clash with each other.
- **Linear as the project memory.** Don't save project progress in Github PR comments or Claude memory. Linear is the single source of truth for project progress.
- **Read every line of code before merge.** After behaviour is proved correct by manual checks in the browser and on phone, push to Github and use the PR system comments to highlight potential issues or reinforce good patterns.
- **Set the tone of challenging ideas.** I didn't want the LLM to act as a yes man and approve any suggestion I made. One of the core behaviours I saved to Claude's memory, was to set ego aside and only focus on the end goal, of easy to read code that implemented the planned feature. During the PR reviews, comments and changes were challenged, some accepted, some modified and some dropped.

The first four days went very well with quick progress through the initial set of issues I had outlined for this milestone in Linear. But halfway through the sprint, I felt my initial plan for the sprint wasn't holding up to what I had learned while implementing those first few features. I took a step back, and with Opus analysed the remaining issues. Several were dropped as either no long relevant, or being a duplicate. I also changed a fundamental design principal of the project, that the clacker is above anything else, an input element. It's usability is the primary goal. That takes precedence over having it behave in a 100% accurate physics simulation, or a single clacker being able to handle multiple axes of movement. It would have been better to have come up with a plan at the start of the sprint that was more robust, and it's something I'll work on for the next sprint.

{% video
   src="/videos/posts/tightening-the-loop/Screencast From 2026-11-03 22-44-59.mp4"
   autoPlay=true
   width="45%" /%}

Within the 8 day window I gave myself, I was able to implement every behaviour and animation I had planned and I really enjoyed the process. I've a solid grasp of the code having reviewed each commit, and found it very focused and efficient to use the PR comments to address any issues I found. Establishing the glossary and sticking to it was also vital, just like in any dev team, and it's something I'll prioritise for any future generative AI projects.

Afterwards I used AI to perform the same meta analysis for the retrospective as last time, but now ironically I used the same model to perform the task. I'm really impressed with Opus 5.5 capabilities, and I trusted it more for the task over Gemini Pro 4. The verdict was some mistakes happened in the first 4 days, but by the end of the sprint, the corrections were based on the feel of the app, and not process based. My conclusion is the difference in result is down to process and not the model itself.

A couple of examples of mistakes encountered was Claude designing for reuse, before a second use actually existed. This involved reusing a method for the turning animation for the spin mode. It was a bad use case as the behaviours have little in common, and was caught during a code review. On my part, for one of the issues, while I was using tailscale on my phone to establish a tmux connection to my pc, I signed off on an issue without first performing the usual behaviour checklist. A follow up issue to perform desktop checks ended up finding issues that were common in both mobile and desktop. This really enforced the principals of sticking the loop of Plan → Commit → Manual Test → Review → Merge, regardless of how I'm feeding prompts to Claude.

My plan is to finish ChromaClacks by converting this prototype to production code in the Sveltekit/Tauri monorepo, along with unit test coverage that I'll put as much effort into designing as the physics and animation functions. Then it'll be converting this basic movement of the clacker to control the colour of the background, and allow it to work as a very over engineered colour picker. It will be a further test of my process to see how this port goes from pure JS/CSS/HTML to the much more complicated structure of the sveltekit/tauri app. It will also be a good test to see if that complexity is worth the ability to run the code as a desktop or mobile app, rather than just a cloud hosted web app.
